CertHub.register({"id":"ccna","vendor":"Cisco","name":"Cisco CCNA","short":"CCNA","exam":"200-301 v2.0","blurb":"Core networking: interfaces and addressing, switching, IP routing, network services and security, plus AI-assisted network operations.","status":"verified","statusNote":"Weights verified Sept 24, 2026 against the CCNA v2.0 exam topics. Use v2.0 unless you are testing before Feb 3, 2027 (v1.1 is the live exam until then).","lastVerified":"2026-09-24","notices":[{"from":"2026-09-24","until":"2027-02-02","text":"Cisco's 200-301 v2.0 starts Feb 3, 2027. If you test before then you'll sit v1.1, which has different topics."}],"examInfo":{"questions":"About 100–120 (Cisco does not publish an exact count)","minutes":120,"pass":"Cisco does not publish a fixed passing score"},"examSim":{"questions":100,"minutes":120},"sources":[{"label":"Cisco CCNA 200-301 exam topics","url":"https://learningnetwork.cisco.com/s/ccna-exam-topics"}],"planWeeks":16,"examDate":"2027-02-08","domains":[{"id":1,"name":"Network infrastructure & connectivity","w":25,"topics":["Diagnose interface and cable issues (copper and fiber): collisions, CRC/input errors, duplex and speed mismatch, distance limits, cable types","Hypervisors (type 1 vs type 2), virtual machines and containers","Network topology architectures: two-tier, three-tier, spine-leaf, WAN, SOHO, on-premises vs cloud","IPv4 addressing and subnetting, including VLSM and private (RFC 1918) ranges","Troubleshoot IPv4 addressing: wrong mask, gateway outside the subnet, duplicate addresses, APIPA","IPv6 address types and prefixes: global unicast, unique local, link-local, multicast, anycast","IPv6 address configuration: static, EUI-64, SLAAC and RA messages; troubleshoot IPv6 addressing","Wireless principles: 2.4, 5 and 6 GHz bands, non-overlapping channels, SSID, RF interference","Troubleshoot wired and wireless client connectivity (verify IP settings on Windows, macOS and Linux)","DHCPv4 on IOS: server pools, excluded addresses, relay with ip helper-address, troubleshooting leases"],"notes":["Exam topics 1.1–1.7","Official v2.0 exam topics PDF, section 1.0"],"labs":["Packet Tracer: cable two switches and a PC, hard-set one side to half duplex, then read show interfaces counters (CRC, runts, late collisions) on both ends and fix the mismatch.","Subnetting drill: carve 192.168.10.0/24 with VLSM for 60, 28, 12 and 2 hosts, then configure the addresses on routers and PCs and ping every gateway.","Configure IPv6 on two routers (one static, one EUI-64), enable ipv6 unicast-routing, and confirm a PC learns its address by SLAAC with show ipv6 interface brief and ipconfig.","Build an IOS DHCP server with excluded addresses, put clients on a different subnet with ip helper-address, and verify with show ip dhcp binding and show ip dhcp conflict."]},{"id":2,"name":"Switching & network access","w":25,"topics":["Switching concepts: MAC learning and aging, frame forwarding, flooding of unknown unicast and broadcast","VLANs (normal range) across multiple switches: access ports, data and voice VLANs, default VLAN","Layer 2 edge-port attributes: VLAN, Power over Ethernet (PoE), port channel and LACP","802.1Q trunking: native VLAN, allowed VLAN lists, DTP modes","Layer 2 discovery protocols: CDP and LLDP","EtherChannel (LACP and static), Layer 2 and Layer 3, and member-port consistency rules","Rapid PVST+: root bridge election, root/designated/alternate ports, port states, PortFast, BPDU guard","Inter-VLAN routing: router-on-a-stick subinterfaces and multilayer switch SVIs","Wireless architectures and AP modes: autonomous, lightweight (CAPWAP), cloud-managed","Troubleshoot VLAN, trunk, EtherChannel and spanning tree problems from show command output"],"notes":["Exam topics section 2.0 (including 2.2: VLAN, PoE, port channel and LACP)","Official v2.0 exam topics PDF, section 2.0"],"labs":["Create VLANs 10, 20 and 99 on two switches, join them with an 802.1Q trunk using native VLAN 99 and a restricted allowed list, and verify with show vlan brief and show interfaces trunk.","Bundle two links with LACP (active/passive), break it with a passive/passive or VLAN mismatch, and read show etherchannel summary before and after the fix.","Force a root bridge with spanning-tree vlan 10 root primary, enable PortFast and BPDU guard on access ports, then plug in a rogue switch and watch the port err-disable.","Route between VLANs twice: router-on-a-stick with dot1Q subinterfaces, then SVIs with ip routing on a multilayer switch; map the lab with show cdp neighbors and show lldp neighbors."]},{"id":3,"name":"IP routing","w":20,"topics":["Routing table components: protocol code, prefix and mask, next hop, administrative distance, metric, gateway of last resort","Forwarding decisions: longest prefix match first, then administrative distance, then metric","IPv4 and IPv6 static routing: default, network, host and floating static routes","Single-area OSPFv2: neighbors and adjacencies, point-to-point vs broadcast networks, DR/BDR election","OSPF router ID selection and configuration (network statements vs interface commands, passive interfaces)","OSPF cost and reference bandwidth","OSPF adjacency requirements: area, subnet, hello/dead timers, MTU, authentication, unique router ID","First hop redundancy: HSRP and VRRP virtual IP, priority, preemption","Troubleshoot IP connectivity with ping, extended ping and traceroute","Troubleshoot OSPF neighbor and route problems from show ip ospf output"],"notes":["Exam topics 3.1–3.8","Official v2.0 exam topics PDF, section 3.0"],"labs":["Three-router Packet Tracer lab: static network routes, a default route and a floating static backup (higher AD); shut the primary link and watch show ip route change.","Single-area OSPFv2 with interface-level ip ospf 1 area 0, manual router IDs and passive LAN interfaces; verify with show ip ospf neighbor and show ip route ospf.","Break OSPF on purpose (mismatched hello timer, area, subnet mask, duplicate router ID), then find each fault using show ip ospf interface and debug ip ospf adj.","Configure HSRP with priority and preempt on two gateways, run a continuous ping from a PC, shut the active router and read show standby brief."]},{"id":4,"name":"Network services & security","w":20,"topics":["AAA for device access: local usernames, TACACS+ and RADIUS clients","Secure management access: SSH version 2, enable secret, VTY access-class, login banners","Standard and extended IPv4 ACLs: wildcard masks, sequence and first match, implicit deny, placement","Layer 2 security: port security (maximum, sticky, violation modes), DHCP snooping, dynamic ARP inspection","NAT and PAT: static, dynamic pool, overload; inside local/global and outside local/global","DHCP and DNS roles in the network; troubleshoot name resolution and DHCP client issues","NTP role in keeping logs and certificates consistent","Wireless security: WPA2 and WPA3, Personal (PSK/SAE) vs Enterprise (802.1X)","VPNs: site-to-site IPsec vs remote-access","Security fundamentals: threats, vulnerabilities, exploits, mitigation and user awareness"],"notes":["Exam topics section 4.0 (including 4.1: local usernames and AAA clients)","Official v2.0 exam topics PDF, section 4.0"],"labs":["Write extended ACLs that stop one subnet from reaching a server over HTTP while allowing ping, place them near the source, and confirm hit counts with show access-lists.","Configure PAT (ip nat inside source list ... overload) for a LAN, browse from two PCs, and read show ip nat translations column by column.","Turn on port security with sticky MACs and violation restrict, then DHCP snooping (trusted uplink) and dynamic ARP inspection; add a rogue DHCP server and watch it get blocked.","Harden a router: SSH v2 only, local AAA login, access-class on the VTY lines, and a TACACS+ or RADIUS server using the Packet Tracer AAA service."]},{"id":5,"name":"AI & network operations/management","w":10,"topics":["AI in network operations: predictive AI and machine learning (anomaly detection, predictive analytics) vs generative AI","Agentic AI in network operations: agents that plan steps and call tools, with guardrails and human approval","Writing prompts for a generative AI system: persona, instructions, data classification, output format","Network management approaches: device-by-device CLI, cloud-managed, controller-based, automation, infrastructure as code","Controller-based networking: management, control and data planes; northbound and southbound APIs","SNMP: manager, agent, MIB, get/set/trap/inform, v2c communities vs v3 security levels","Configuration management with Ansible: agentless, SSH, YAML playbooks, inventory, idempotency","Syslog: message format, severity levels 0–7, facilities, logging to a server","Telemetry and AIOps: streaming telemetry vs polling, baselines and event correlation"],"notes":["Exam topics 5.1–5.6","Official v2.0 exam topics PDF, section 5.0"],"labs":["Configure SNMPv3 (authPriv) and syslog to a server in Packet Tracer, set logging trap warnings, then generate link and config events and note which ones arrive.","Install Ansible in a Linux VM, build an inventory of CML or lab routers, and run a cisco.ios playbook that sets a banner and a VLAN; run it twice and compare changed vs ok.","Write three prompts asking a generative AI assistant to summarize a show logging dump: add a persona, clear instructions, redacted data and a required output format, and compare the results.","Explore a free Cisco DevNet sandbox (Catalyst Center or Meraki dashboard) and list which tasks the controller centralizes compared with device-by-device CLI."]}],"study":{"1":[["Subnet 172.16.0.0/16 into /22 networks. How many subnets, how many usable hosts each, and what is the fifth subnet's range and broadcast?","/22 borrows 6 bits: 2^6 = 64 subnets, 2^10 − 2 = 1022 hosts each. Block size 4 in the third octet: 0, 4, 8, 12, 16. The fifth is 172.16.16.0/22, hosts 172.16.16.1–172.16.19.254, broadcast 172.16.19.255."],["Host 10.1.37.200/27: find the network, broadcast and usable range.","/27 = block of 32 in the last octet (0, 32, …, 192, 224). 200 falls in 192–223, so network 10.1.37.192, broadcast 10.1.37.223, hosts .193–.222."],["Compress 2001:0db8:0000:0000:0a00:0000:0000:0001 correctly.","Drop leading zeros and replace one run of zero groups with ::. Both zero runs are two groups long, so compress the first: 2001:db8::a00:0:0:1."],["Build the EUI-64 address for MAC 0050.56AB.CDEF on prefix 2001:db8:1:1::/64.","Split the MAC, insert FFFE (0050:56FF:FEAB:CDEF), flip the 7th bit of the first byte (00 → 02): 2001:db8:1:1:250:56ff:feab:cdef."],["What show interfaces symptoms point to a duplex mismatch, and how do you fix it?","The half-duplex side logs late collisions; the full-duplex side logs CRC errors and runts, and throughput is poor while the link stays up. Set both ends to auto (or both to the same fixed speed and duplex)."],["Compare a type 1 hypervisor, a type 2 hypervisor and containers.","Type 1 runs on bare metal (ESXi, Hyper-V); type 2 runs on top of a host OS (VirtualBox, Workstation). Containers share the host kernel and package only the app and its dependencies, so they are lighter than VMs."],["A DHCP client is on VLAN 20 and the DHCP server is on another subnet. Walk through DORA and the command that makes it work.","Discover, Offer, Request, Acknowledge. Discover is a broadcast, so the VLAN 20 gateway needs ip helper-address <server IP> to relay it as unicast to the server."]],"2":[["How is the STP root bridge elected, and what is the default priority for VLAN 10?","Lowest bridge ID wins: priority first, then MAC address. Priority is 32768 plus the VLAN ID (extended system ID), so the default for VLAN 10 is 32778."],["Which LACP and PAgP mode combinations form a channel?","LACP: active–active or active–passive. PAgP: desirable–desirable or desirable–auto. Passive–passive and auto–auto never form; on–on forms a static channel with no negotiation."],["What does a switch do with a frame whose destination MAC is not in the MAC table?","It floods the frame out every port in the same VLAN except the one it arrived on, and learns the source MAC on the ingress port."],["Why use PortFast together with BPDU guard on access ports?","PortFast moves the port straight to forwarding so hosts get DHCP quickly. BPDU guard err-disables the port if a BPDU arrives, stopping someone from plugging in a switch and creating a loop or root takeover."],["List the router-on-a-stick configuration for VLANs 10 and 20.","On the router: interface g0/0.10, encapsulation dot1Q 10, ip address for VLAN 10; repeat for .20. On the switch, make the port facing the router an 802.1Q trunk that allows VLANs 10 and 20."],["What must match on member ports before they can bundle into one EtherChannel?","Speed, duplex, switchport mode (access or trunk), access VLAN or allowed/native VLANs, and the negotiation protocol. A mismatch leaves ports suspended or stand-alone."],["A PoE access point on Gi0/7 stays dark. What do you check?","show power inline: confirm the port is not set to power inline never, that the switch power budget is not exhausted, and that the AP's class fits what the port can supply."]],"3":[["A router has 10.0.0.0/8, 10.1.0.0/16 and 10.1.1.0/24 in its table. Which route is used for 10.1.1.77 and for 10.1.2.5?","Longest prefix match: 10.1.1.77 uses 10.1.1.0/24; 10.1.2.5 does not match the /24, so it uses 10.1.0.0/16."],["List the default administrative distances you need for CCNA.","Connected 0, static 1, eBGP 20, internal EIGRP 90, OSPF 110, RIP 120, external EIGRP 170, iBGP 200. Lower is more trusted."],["Summarize 192.168.16.0/24 through 192.168.23.0/24 into one route.","16 = 00010000 and 23 = 00010111 share the first 5 bits of the third octet, so 16 + 5 = 21 bits: 192.168.16.0/21 (255.255.248.0)."],["How is the OSPF DR elected on a broadcast segment?","Highest interface priority wins, then highest router ID. Priority 0 means never DR or BDR. The election is not preemptive, so a better router joining later does not take over."],["Why do Fast Ethernet and Gigabit links look the same to OSPF by default, and how do you fix it?","Cost = reference bandwidth / interface bandwidth with a 100 Mbps reference, so anything 100 Mbps or faster costs 1. Set auto-cost reference-bandwidth (for example 100000) on every router."],["How do you build a floating static route that backs up an OSPF-learned default?","Give the static route an AD higher than 110, for example ip route 0.0.0.0 0.0.0.0 198.51.100.1 200. It stays out of the table until the OSPF route disappears."],["HSRP vs VRRP: what differs?","HSRP is Cisco-proprietary (active/standby, default priority 100, preempt off by default). VRRP is an open standard (master/backup, preempt on by default) and the virtual IP can be a real interface address."]],"4":[["Write the wildcard mask for 172.16.32.0/20 and give the range it matches.","/20 mask 255.255.240.0 → wildcard 0.0.15.255. Block size 16 in the third octet, so it matches 172.16.32.0–172.16.47.255."],["Where do you place standard and extended ACLs, and why?","Extended ACLs go close to the source because they match on destination and port, so they drop only the unwanted traffic early. Standard ACLs match only the source, so place them close to the destination to avoid blocking too much."],["Compare TACACS+ and RADIUS.","TACACS+: Cisco, TCP 49, encrypts the whole payload, separates authentication, authorization and accounting (good for device admin). RADIUS: open standard, UDP 1812/1813, encrypts only the password, combines authn and authz (good for network access and 802.1X)."],["Explain the four NAT address terms using a PAT example.","Inside local = the private host address (192.168.1.10). Inside global = the public address it is translated to (203.0.113.5). Outside global = the real remote address; outside local = how that remote host appears inside (usually the same)."],["How do DHCP snooping and DAI work together?","DHCP snooping drops server messages on untrusted ports and builds a binding table of MAC, IP, VLAN and port. DAI checks ARP packets on untrusted ports against that table and drops spoofed ARP."],["What do the port security violation modes protect, restrict and shutdown do?","Protect drops frames from unknown MACs silently. Restrict drops them, logs, sends SNMP traps and increments the violation counter. Shutdown (default) err-disables the port."]],"5":[["List the syslog severity levels in order.","0 Emergency, 1 Alert, 2 Critical, 3 Error, 4 Warning, 5 Notification, 6 Informational, 7 Debugging. Setting a level logs that level and everything more severe (lower number)."],["What does SNMPv3 add over SNMPv2c?","Users instead of plain-text community strings, with three security levels: noAuthNoPriv, authNoPriv (message authentication) and authPriv (authentication plus encryption)."],["Give one predictive AI and one generative AI use case in network operations.","Predictive/ML: learn baselines and flag anomalies or forecast link saturation before it happens. Generative: draft a config snippet, summarize logs or write a change-ticket description for human review."],["What makes a good prompt for a generative AI system in operations work?","A persona (\"act as a network engineer\"), clear instructions and scope, only data that is allowed to be shared (remove secrets and sensitive data per classification), and a required output format such as a table or bullet list."],["What is agentic AI and what guardrails does it need in a network?","An AI that plans multi-step tasks and calls tools or APIs to act on them. It needs least-privilege access, change approval by a human, logging of every action and a rollback path."],["Why is Ansible described as agentless and idempotent?","Agentless: it connects over SSH (or an API) with nothing installed on the device. Idempotent: running the same playbook again changes nothing if the device already matches the desired state."]]},"final":{"topics":["Two full timed practice exams (100 questions, 120 minutes)","Subnetting and IPv6 speed drills: aim for under 60 seconds per problem","Re-lab your weakest domain from show command output alone","Review every missed question and its exam topic number"],"lab":"Rebuild a full campus in Packet Tracer from scratch in one sitting: VLANs, trunks, EtherChannel, OSPF, HSRP, DHCP relay, NAT, ACLs, SSH and syslog."},"qCount":83,"hasLessons":true,"hasLessonsEs":true,"hasPbqs":true});
